A||||43||DreXteR||3.4 TDC makes 281HP!!??||||4||
Z||000000||DreXteR||02-14-2001||10:43 AM||||Hi all, well I know this topic has been here before. But I have a few ideas for it, Im not an expert thats why this topic. <P>K lets start, I read this info about the 3.4 TDC  <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/ca2/34Performance/dohc.html</A> . In here he said that the real power of this engine is 281HP! And also said that 'cause of a poor tranny handling power they decied to lower the power to 200.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  And the powers was decreased by a program in the ECM. If this is true, there is a possible way to get there?.. Well, by all that he wrote, he give a lot of detail. Where he get that info? Hmmm... anyway, all relies in the computer, fuel/air, spark advance or decrease, DIS ingition, O2, and other stuff. If this is true(281hp), GM will change any design of this engine to reduce the power? hmmm...  This is an interesting point. If they decide to do it, it will take more time to take the cars in to the market. And the factor of the story was time!! So I think not, they leave the engine design intact. <P>What could they do to lower the power? ... The program in the ECM makes sence to me, but not only that could make it.  Maybe a combination of things like fuel pressure regulator, injectors, Fuel/Air mix, timing, DIS, maybe cams. <P>Looking for stuff @ carparts I found some HKS electrinics that maybe can make the job. found this <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com/parts/newsearch2.asp?reflink=53694-0-0-0-0&sourceid=2&parttypeid=3054&itemid=7870&autoidsearch=YES" TARGET=_blank>Vein Pressure Converter</A>. and some other things form [url=http://www.carparts.com/parts/newsearch1.asp?sourceid=2&autoidsearch=yes&reflink=53694-0-0-0-0&SegmentId=3&parttypeid=3054&vehicleid=53694&catalogid=&partnum=NONE&dc=]hks[/url ]. Maybe using this the 281hp can be reached!(if it its true).  What are your coments of this? I know that in here are a lot of people with knowledge beyond mine. Lets figure it out!!  <P>Another Issue, in his article the engine has forged everithing! Its this is true? (Compared to the real existing engines). The DIS is controlled from the ECM, this can be a real pain to optimize. MSD makes a part(not sure what is) that is DIS related. This can control the DIS ignition instead of the ECM?<P>Someone have more info about this?<P>Thanx<P>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Phil||02-14-2001||03:13 PM||||Forum members have hashed this subject before and the concensis was that it's another "handling by Lotus" deal. Maybe with a turbo or super charger but not with just a chip change.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||02-14-2001||03:28 PM||||True, but even if it didn't have 281 (which I am pretty sure it didn't), you could get by switching cams, injectors, pumps like he was saying (I assume you could get that much)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||02-14-2001||03:28 PM||||The 4t80e tranny holds up to a 300 hp North* and doesn't seem to have many durability issues. There are forum members who are very good at hacking GM ecm/pcm's and will probably tell you that GM does not leave any hidden horse power in the chip. ||||reg||13
Z||000004||DreXteR||02-14-2001||03:53 PM||||I dont mean getting there just with a chip.  <B>If its true</B>, track down the possibles ways that can be done. Its like reverse engineering, thats the way to crack programs from its trial version to a retail one.<P>I dont know much of how ECM works or has to be programmed, but I know how to make programs for computer, and understand dif behaviors.  Maybe the kind of program that the computer have dont let aftermarket chips take full advantage of what the engine can do.  This is like a challenge research. <P>BTW, I read a thread from a Beretta prototype 2.8 twin turbo that makes 383HP!! Thats way to much for a normal turbo setup can be achieved(4 my knowledge), from 120-130 to 383 its a loooong way. But that was in a scanned article from somewhere, not some page write from a fan. <P>Check in supercars.net , there are some prototypes with such incredible power that I dont beleave it. Explains this <A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=2000&m=Toyota&o=Ultimate%20Celica%20Concept" TARGET=_blank>Celica Porwer</A>. Why an 3.1 <A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=2000&m=Acura&o=NSX-T" TARGET=_blank>Acura NSX-T</A> makes 290hp na? still being a 3.1 v6. The <A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=2001&m=BMW&o=Z3%20M%20Roadster" TARGET=_blank>Z3-M</A> make 315hp na. What I saying is, there can be a possibility that the info of 3.4tdc can make 281hp can be true. Maybe is not just a chip, maybe there something else. <P>Lets find out!<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000005||DreXteR||02-14-2001||04:04 PM||||BTW, check this out!<BR>This is a <A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=1999&m=Nissan&o=Skyline%20GT-R%20R34" TARGET=_blank>1999 Nissan Skyline GT-R R34</A> makes 276hp and its twin brother <A HREF="http://www.supercars.net/SDBQ?y=1999&m=Nissan%20Blitz&o=Skyline%20GT-R%20R348" TARGET=_blank>1999 Nissan Blitz Skyline GT-R R348</A> pumping 850hp!.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-14-2001||06:39 PM||||The 281HP was done with compression and cams. I recall reading an SAE paper some time ago that mentioned this. The folks on the TDC board mention it from time to time. <P>There is a company in FL (I believe) that sells a set of cams for $1000-ish. Whether or not that would get you to 281HP is unknown. With those cams having to change the calibrations is a moot point. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||loafer87gt||02-14-2001||07:00 PM||||That other skyline is probably make a helluva a lot more HP than 276. That is the max horsepower a car can have to be sold in Japan. If you look at the 300 ZX, Supra, and Skyline, you will see them all rated at 276 horsepower over there. The skylines a sweet ride tho, give my left nut for one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||02-14-2001||08:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>  <P>Another Issue, in his article the engine has forged everithing! Its this is true? (Compared to the real existing engines). The DIS is controlled from the ECM, this can be a real pain to optimize. MSD makes a part(not sure what is) that is DIS related. This can control the DIS ignition instead of the ECM?<P>Someone have more info about this?<P>Thanx<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've looked in the shop manual. The connecting rods are forged, but the crank is cast. I'm not sure if the pistons are cast or hypereutectic, but they're not forged.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pontiaddict||02-14-2001||09:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>BTW, I read a thread from a Beretta prototype 2.8 twin turbo that makes 383HP!! Thats way to much for a normal turbo setup can be achieved(4 my knowledge), from 120-130 to 383 its a loooong way. But that was in a scanned article from somewhere, not some page write from a fan. <P>DreX</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That motor was built by GM for a test mule. Had LOTS of work done to it. Not too many stock parts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DreXteR||02-14-2001||09:38 PM||||Thanks ppl. <P>1000 for a set of cams! :ekk: I can buy an entire engine for that. <P>Well, about the hks electrinics???? Some 1 have a idea?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GTZ34||02-14-2001||10:19 PM||||Dre, I would like to think all in that article is factual but... I just finished tearing my 3.4 dow last week and the crank is cas iron and the pistons are aluminum (but sturdy).  The eng would hold up to mild Nos but I don't think you could continually bang on it and have it hold together.  The crank is stout by design, but I am getting forged pistons and rods for the beast as I plan on a severe duty eng.  Also, if you plan on taking it past the 6.5k rev limit, something needs to be done on the top end with regards to springs & keepers.  I am currently trying to find a solution on that one.  So if the crank and piston statements don't match my eng, I don't know about the 281.  However, that number is not unachievable with an open tab at a machine shop...||||reg||1
Z||000012||gabriel||02-15-2001||12:28 AM||||Look dudes.<P>   I'm new to all the electronics ECM/PCM emission control, ect, et. al, ad. nauseum stuff, but I have wrenched, raced & studied quite a while.<P>  No way a chip on a mildly setup engine is going to turn that engine into a fire breathing 280 - 300+ HP monster.<P>  Terryk is right.  Had to be done w/ compression & cam duration/overlap.  And then modified exhast. And modified intake. And prolly modified heads & triple ground valves seats.  Modified valve springs & such as well.<P>Not to mention timing curves & fuel mixtures to match, as well as the powertrain that is going to survive this type of power.  <P>  Then there are the issues to keep this engine intact.  Forged everything?  Just the start.  Think hot tanked, magnafluxed, line bored, decked, balanced & blueprinted, steel crank, modified journals, etc, etc.  <P>   I believe I could (ECM issues aside) assemble/ machine a 3.4 to output 300hp+ without a great deal of problems (although not without a great deal of $$$$$) but how many hours of engine use would_it_last?<P>   If you are going for straight out linear acceleration on the 5280 thats one thing.<P>  Making it last on the road is another.<P>  NOS is prolly the cheapest way to improve HP & performance on an intermittent basis.<P>    IMHO, chasing HP on the street means cubic inches.  Period.<P>   *Cinches up flameproof welders suit*<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>850HP??  For what?  One run?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||02-15-2001||01:11 AM||||With DOHC 4 bangers putting out close to 200 or more h/p stock, is it really that hard to believe that a DOHC 3.4l v6 could put out 281?  With almost twice the displacement of the 4's you'll have torque to back it up, too.  GM FWD trannies have always been weak, and they have warrenty issues to consider.  I've heard what Terry said, compression and cam changes to take out power.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||DreXteR||02-15-2001||10:28 AM||||Cams and compression makes sense. But, maybe another little things have to be optimized like fuel system and DIS. I have a strong feeling that those 2 is part of the problem. I read in somewere that some vehicles have a fuel limiter, making run lean @high rpms.<P>The haks products have some parts that can fix this kinds of problems.||||reg||4
Z||000015||1FST2M6||02-15-2001||10:35 AM||||look for -  msweldon  - Marc has a 3.4TDC... and LOADS of information... <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Will||02-15-2001||03:09 PM||||Getting power out of an engine is just a matter of spinning it fast. The new Ferrari 3.6 litre V8 makes 395 HP, but that peak power comes right at the engine's 8500 (!) RPM redline.<P>I think 340-350 HP from a 3.4 TDC redlined at 8000 RPM or above  would be possible, but you'd need cams, valvesprings, and a complete rebuild, of the likes of which Gabriel spoke.<P>THINK people! This is GM we're talking about here! The thing HAS to share cam blanks--or at least basic cam dimensions--with something else GM makes! Who knows, it might even share valves and springs!||||reg||1
Z||000017||DreXteR||02-15-2001||03:43 PM||||Interesting point you have there Will. Other engine using a dif but shared dimension cams....<P>||||reg||3
Z||000018||lou_dias||02-15-2001||04:29 PM||||Think people.  You're only as strong at your bottom end.  These high priced cars that are spinning to XXXXX RPM are using different materials to make their bottom end.  Titanium, titanium, titanium.<P>Now go price titanium pistons, crank and rods.  Carbon fiber bearings.  That's how you know you can make power.  Spin it as fast as you want knowing it won't break.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Will||02-15-2001||08:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>Think people.  You're only as strong at your bottom end.  These high priced cars that are spinning to XXXXX RPM are using different materials to make their bottom end.  Titanium, titanium, titanium.<P>Now go price titanium pistons, crank and rods.  Carbon fiber bearings.  That's how you know you can make power.  Spin it as fast as you want knowing it won't break.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>NASCAR spins small block chevy's past 9000 without exotic technology, just good engine building.<P>The 3.4 TDC can use slightly modified small block rods, just like the 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 pushrod. With a good set of forged or billet rods (it comes with forged rods and will wind past 7K stock, remember?), forged pistons, forged crank, and quality bearings it should have no trouble winding to 8K+ or 8K++, provided the heads, cams, and valvesprings can tolerate that RPM and flow enough air to make decent power.<P>The box-stock bottom end of a Northstar will spin to 8K. The only thing that holds it back is that the stock valvesprings float at 6800.||||reg||1
Z||000020||bubbajoexx||02-15-2001||09:54 PM||||you guys are all talking about hp . HP is nothing if you do not have the torque to put it to the ground sure you can make 850 hp out of a 1.5 litre formula one engine but remeber they have a fist gear ratio of 6.75 that is needed to even get the car moving and 20000 rpm to develope the hp with a max torque out put of 260 ft lbs at 11000 rpm not to good for street use develope torque first then increse the hp ||||reg||1
Z||000021||terryk||02-15-2001||10:03 PM||||...ergo a stock DOHC won't put out 281HP....||||reg||12
Z||000022||87GTZ34||02-15-2001||10:08 PM||||Forged rods aren't a problem as its easy to piggy back off of old style SBC.  The chevy performance guide says the crank is stout and should handle revs to race levels if the center 2 mains are cross drilled and the crank is race prepped (no brainer).  Although I haven't looked everywhere, I have not found any forged cranks or pistons for the 3.4 DOHC.  Has anyone here found them?||||reg||1
Z||000023||terryk||02-15-2001||10:42 PM||||I believe the 3.1L Turbo GP engine used a forged crank. But then I believe a lot of things.||||reg||1
Z||000024||DJRice||02-15-2001||11:16 PM||||Its been said before, and it will get said again.  The power in the engine is directly proportional to the amount of money you spend on it.  Could you develop 281HP from a 3.4L???  Sure, but it might cost $10K.  On the other hand you could spend half that on an sweet SBC motor and an Archie kit and easily break the 300HP (if not 350HP) mark.  (The other half you could dump into the transmission, because of that good old V8 torque) <P>Possible and cost effective are two different things.......<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000025||terryk||02-15-2001||11:50 PM||||Third base!||||reg||1
Z||000026||msweldon||02-16-2001||12:24 AM||||Hello everyone,<P>I've been studying the 3.4 DOHC for many years, just off and on until recently when I actually bought one. I have long thought the 3.4 was the engine that should've gone into the fiero. The 3.4TDC has all the right ingredients for a 281+hp factory mule, unlocking it, however, IS the problem.  To me a ~280 hp NA V6 in a GT is the "Perfect" car, well maybe with an interior redesign.  I've reasearched the HELL out of this engine for quite some time and have found MANY interesting sources and data.<P>1.) Put the engine specs into a DYNO simulation application, even the one's used at GM, and a 3.4V6 with heads that flow as well the TDC's actually equates to just over 300hp "possible."  <P>2.) I've spoken in detail to the author of the article at the Angelfire website.  He is an ASE tech and knows one of the original TDC engineers from where he got HIS info on the web-site.  He KNOWS his stuff....and is quite disgruntled with motor-heads such as ourselves bothering him about the 281hp rosetta stone.  The only way I've been able to discuss the 3.4TDC with him is by agreeing to document the entire project of reaching the 280+hp TDC and turning it over to public domain so people will quit bothering him.<P>3.)He's shown me the fuel table runs on a TDC and the engine does "thin" out towards the upper rpms due to the ECM.  Not 50-70hp worth, but very noticeable.<P>4.) The cams on the engine are rather mild and are a good compromise for both low-end torque with some high-end hp.  I've found on DYNO2000 as has lowcg, with a good bit more duration and some lift the engine responds really well.  Not 50-70hp worth, but very noticeable<P>5.) Micheal Smith and Mike Matter out in CA have a Fiero-TDC mailing list about improving the TDC as much as possible.  Micheal Smith has been able to squeeze 10 or so horses out of his 96'DOHC with "simple" cam timing and ECM changes.  He has also successfully rev'd his DOHC upto 7600 rpm using .030" shims under his stock valve springs.  The stock valves are just too soft.  He just recently swapped in Q4 aftermarket springs, but these were WAY to stiff for the belt drive cam system.<P>6.) Compression seems to be another area to eek out a few more hp's as well, a head milling job or even going with North* pistons would raise the CP to ~10.5:1.  Yet again a little more gain. Not 50-70hp but very noticeable.<P>7.)Ford's Yamaha SHO V6 originally put out 300hp using variable intake runner technology on a 3.0L, but was scaled down due to weak trannies as well.  Honda/Acura's NSX 3.0 - 3.2L V6 using VTECH does 276 - 295hp.  GM's, in comparison "relatively low-tech" 3.4 TDC did it with cubic inches and unbelievable head design, once you see the TDC heads in relation to the Cast-block you can definately tell, "somethings special about these heads"<P>My plan is to first, get the damn thing installed into my 87GT using an Aluminum flywheel and kevlar clutch from the fiero store and replace the stock ECM with a Haltech unit, BIG learning curve I'm sure, not mention big bucks.  Then I should have the necessary doo-dads to play with the fuel curves and spark advance to possibly unlock "that" area and then move onto the cams and valve springs.<P>Maybe I'll reach the rosetta stone, maybe I won't, but It'll be fun and if I'm unsuccessful, well I can always goto Engineered Performance and get 1FST2M6 and the gang in Smyrna to pop-in a nice turbo system.  But if i reach that magic hp number, I'll be one happy fiero owner and we'll ALL know how it was done here on Pennocks.<P>I have much more info on all this and will give up as much as possible as time allows.<P>Software Engineering takes up WAY too much time, good money but no damn time to spend it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That's where the fiero comes in, heheheh<P>later fellas (and ladies),<BR>Marc (msweldon)||||reg||1
Z||000027||lowCG||02-16-2001||01:27 AM||||Yeah,this month's Hotrod mag had an article on the Quad four motors,and it made it aparent that the TDC is in fact mildly tuned in comparison as far as cams and exhaust are concerned. <BR>Terry ,what's the name of the place with the cams?<BR>Was actually thinking about modifying the intake manifold,and adding a larger throttle body/airflow meter,but am not sure if it would yield all that much.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||msweldon||02-16-2001||10:26 AM||||Ditto LowCG||||reg||1
Z||000029||msweldon||02-16-2001||10:27 AM||||Ditto LowCG<P>Terry, where was the name of that CAM place in FL.  sounds interesting...<P>marc||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||02-16-2001||11:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by msweldon:<BR><B>Hello everyone,<BR>3)... Not 50-70hp worth, but very noticeable.<P>4)... Not 50-70hp worth, but very noticeable<P>6)... Not 50-70hp but very noticeable.<P><BR>My plan is to first, get the damn thing installed into my 87GT using an Aluminum flywheel and kevlar clutch from the fiero store and replace the stock ECM with a Haltech unit, BIG learning curve I'm sure, not mention big bucks.  Then I should have the necessary doo-dads to play with the fuel curves and spark advance to possibly unlock "that" area and then move onto the cams and valve springs.<P><BR>Software Engineering takes up WAY too much time, good money but no damn time to spend it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  That's where the fiero comes in, heheheh<P>later fellas (and ladies),<BR>Marc (msweldon)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As the saying goes, "If you need 10 HP, you won't find 10 HP in one place, but you might find 1 HP in 10 places."<P>Keep up the good work. How do the crank and cam position sensors from the TDC compare with those of the modern 3400 SFI?<P>Terryk: what's the name of that place in FL? Do they have a website?||||reg||1
Z||000031||terryk||02-16-2001||11:23 AM||||I was looking for it last night. Seems Mike Smith mentioned it one time too. I'll try to find it. Matters might know.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000032||DreXteR||02-16-2001||10:39 PM||||I found an info of someone that have a 3.4 tdc an talk about the performance cams, 250 each.<P> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/gm34ldohc/performance.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/gm34ldohc/performance.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||terryk||02-17-2001||12:08 AM||||Sorry guys. It seems that's where I saw it and I read into it something different than what Nick meant. He was saying that a new grind from blanks is possible, not that he found cams. I'm sure GM would tell you who makes the cam blanks. It's one of the big guys (Comp, TRW, Iksy, etc.) You can then spend the money on grinding.<P>I don't think base grinding would work. Maybe Matters or Smith can shed some light on that.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||terryk||02-17-2001||08:32 PM||||Paeco Industry High Performance quoted him $275/cam. Try them.<P>Terry ||||reg||1
Z||000035||ray b||02-17-2001||08:51 PM||||are the v6 3.4 and newer 3.5 same heads?<BR>new isusu SUV uses 3.5 at rated 235hp.<BR>crsfab has N* hotrod cams and better lifters<BR>maybe they can cut cams for 3.4 or 3.5 motors.<BR>will lifter inter change with 3.4?||||reg||1
Z||000036||Matt Hawkins||02-18-2001||09:37 AM||||The 3.4l and 3.5l engines are completely different.  The 3.5l is a V6 version of the northstar.  Sorry, no interchanging parts.||||reg||1
Z||000037||lou_dias||02-18-2001||04:53 PM||||Will, they also never run the same engine twice and keep a spare just in case.<BR>I'm sure I can by a chip to let my engine spin to 7000rpms with factory rods (ignoring valve float here), but if I do this everyday, my engine would be shot in a matter of weeks.  That's where the need for better materials comes in.  Durability and consistency is what you want in a steet car.||||reg||1
Z||000038||ray b||02-18-2001||08:06 PM||||can N* lifters be used on 3.5 then? valve springss?<BR>is 3.5 same V60 deg and firing order as 3.4?<BR>can 3.4 OBD-1 computer run 3.5 motor?<BR>I like 3.5 for lite weight but don't want to mess with OBD-2.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||BillHarrison||02-18-2001||08:14 PM||||On the 3.5 subject...  I work for Olds/Buick/Pontiac, and have checked out the 3.5  Parts wise, it has alot of unique parts, although alot of them are shared with the northstar engine family.  Currently the engine is only available in the Oldsmobile Intrigue and the 01 Aurora, but GM has it slated to eventually replace the 3.8 completely across the line.  IMHO, it is a nice engine, having driven several Intrigues equipped with it.  Moves a 3500 pound car with some authority.  Might make a good Fiero powerplant!  Alot of work there though!||||reg||1
Z||000040||Will||02-18-2001||11:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>Will, they also never run the same engine twice and keep a spare just in case.<BR>I'm sure I can by a chip to let my engine spin to 7000rpms with factory rods (ignoring valve float here), but if I do this everyday, my engine would be shot in a matter of weeks.  That's where the need for better materials comes in.  Durability and consistency is what you want in a steet car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know that racing engines don't live long lives. Formula 1 engines are made of pretty fancy stuff, but they get rebuilt between races, too. The difference is that the racing team has nothing to do with engine rebuilds; the engines go back to the factory for that.<BR>My point was that fancy materials aren't necessary, just good engine building skills.<P>If your car wound to 7000, how often would you actually use it? I don't think an occasional 7K sprint would destroy a TDC anyway. Why are you concerned about factory rods? Didn't I already mention that those engines have forged rods from the factory?||||reg||1
Z||000041||Will||02-18-2001||11:15 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>can N* lifters be used on 3.5 then? valve springss?<BR>is 3.5 same V60 deg and firing order as 3.4?<BR>can 3.4 OBD-1 computer run 3.5 motor?<BR>I like 3.5 for lite weight but don't want to mess with OBD-2.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 3.5--as all v6's derived from v8's--is a 90 degree engine. Check out <A HREF="http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/35_main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpowertrain.com/engines_cartruck/other/35_main.htm</A>    for info on the 3.5. It does share the 1-2-3-4-5-6 firing order of the 60 degree engines, though.<BR>The 3.5 came out in '95, I think, and probably wasn't OBDII until '96.<BR>If you really want to know about the 3.5, call <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com" TARGET=_blank>CHRFab</A> and ask if they've ever messed with one.<P>BillHarrison: does the 3.5 use the same crankshaft and camshaft position sensors as the Northstar?<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||lou_dias||02-19-2001||09:14 AM||||Will, the original point was to spin our "stock" engines faster to get more power.  My point is that these engines as they stand can't consistently spin that fast and live a long life.  So...<BR>To get 281 horses out of the factory 3.4 TDC, I'm assuming you are going to want to feel this much power often enough to seriously shorten the engine life unless you invest in the better quality parts.  A 281 hp 3.4 @ 8000 rpms isn't going to have much of a streetable torque range for daily driving.<P>I think it's better, cheaper, and simpler to just turbo a 3.4 pushrod engine to 300 horses @ 5000rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000043||Will||02-19-2001||11:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>Will, the original point was to spin our "stock" engines faster to get more power.  My point is that these engines as they stand can't consistently spin that fast and live a long life.  So...<BR>To get 281 horses out of the factory 3.4 TDC, I'm assuming you are going to want to feel this much power often enough to seriously shorten the engine life unless you invest in the better quality parts.  A 281 hp 3.4 @ 8000 rpms isn't going to have much of a streetable torque range for daily driving.<P>I think it's better, cheaper, and simpler to just turbo a 3.4 pushrod engine to 300 horses @ 5000rpm.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you look back and read my post, I wrote words to the effect of "...<I>build</I> a 3.4 TDC to spin to 8K+..."  I am under no illusion that a stock 3.4 TDC short block would last long spun to 8K+ on a regular basis.<BR>However, the short block should easily handle 7K.<BR>BTW, if you do the math, a built TDC should be able to make 350 HP at 8K, provided the camdrive holds up.<BR>280 should be very easy at 7K. It shold be just a matter of a cam a valvespring change (that sounds oddly like what the factory did to detune it....)||||reg||1
